WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:03.380
The massive migration of wildebeests

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:04.640
across the Serengeti

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.720
has a huge impact on the ecosystem.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.960
One overlooked aspect

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.360
has been what happens

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.940
when the wildebeests drown

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.320
during river crossings

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.060
Every year, 1.2 million wildebeests

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.840
move across the savannah,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.160
consuming more than 4,500 tons

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.980
of grass every day

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.620
in an 1800-kilometer circuit

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.420
between Kenya and Tanzania

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.960
as they follow the rains.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.820
Their herding instinct is strong because

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.480
even though being in a crowd

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.640
leads to stiff competition for food,

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.420
this part of Africa has so many lions

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.060
&amp; other predators that strength in numbers wins out—

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:44.540
even if it means sometimes

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.160
blindly swimming toward death.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.580
Almost annually, thousands drown in river crossings,

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.140
often because the opposite banks are too steep or

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.300
too many are trying to cross at one time.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.680
For the past six years,

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.120
researchers have taken stock

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.380
of the pileup of carcasses,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.040
surveyed the parade of scavengers

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.040
assisting in their decomposition,

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.560
and used modeling and chemical analyses

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.880
to track where nutrients provided by

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.920
these dead animals wind up in the food chain.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.520
About 6500 animals drown each year,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.500
dumping the equivalent of 10 blue whale carcasses

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.180
into the river.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.360
Half of that biomass is bones,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.400
which take 7 years to decay

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.780
Carcasses accumulate at

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.400
bends in the river and outcroppings

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.140
Annually they add about 13 tons of phosphorous,

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.380
25 tons of nitrogen

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.020
and 107 tons of carbon to the ecosystem

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.640
The nutrients come in pulses

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.500
that last about a month each

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.120
and can temporarily double,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.560
or even quadruple some nutrient levels.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.600
Vultures and storks flock to the site for the feast,

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.060
and likely move wildebeest derived nutrients

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.860
inland after they fly away

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.360
Crocodiles, too, are known to congregate.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.900
Chemical analyses showed

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.460
that up to 50% of the diet of local fish

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.120
comes from the carcasses when they're present.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.880
After the meat disappears,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.880
the remaining bones begin to support

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.300
a film of microbes

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.760
that in turn become food for fish

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.060
and other river-dwellers.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:20.300
On the positive side,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.540
the wildebeest drownings

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.720
only involve about about 0.7% of the total herd

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.440
Illegal harvesting, starvation and predation

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.440
take many more.

